WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Step three of our compression skills is rate and rhythm.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:12.000
To keep blood moving effectively to the brain, you need to maintain a rate of 100 to 120 compressions per minute.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Now, in a high stress situation, it can be really hard to judge exactly how fast that is.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
A great trick is to use music.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
You've probably heard people mention songs like Stayin' Alive or Baby Shark.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Any song with a steady beat between 100 and 120 beats per minute works perfectly.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Counting out loud from 1 to 30 as you work also helps you stay on track and keeps everyone around you informed of your progress.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.000
As you push at this speed, there is one more vital step, full chest recoil.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Recoil simply means release.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.000
After every single push down, you must lift your hands just enough to let the chest spring all the way back up to its normal position.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Think of it like squeezing a sponge.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.000
You squeeze to push the water out, but you have to let go completely to let the sponge expand so it can soak up more water.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.000
The heart works in a similar way.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
If you don't let the chest come all the way back up, the heart doesn't have time to refill with blood,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.000
and your next compression won't have anything to pump out.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Watch your instructor now as they demonstrate a full set of 30 compressions.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Pay attention to that steady rhythm and how the chest fully resets between every single push.

